#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186618044 :question/problem "Copy and complete the table below and use it to determine which of the following is the graph of $y=-x+2.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "First, we fill the table. Given a value of $x,$ we can find the corresponding $y$ by substituting $x$ into the formula $y=-x+2.$ Therefore,\n\n- if $x=0$ then $y=-(0)+2=2,$\n\n- if $x=1$ then $y=-(1)+2=1,$\n\n- if $x=2$ then $y=-(2)+2=0,$ and\n\n- if $x=3$ then $y=-(3)+2=-1.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $2$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-1$ |\n\nWe plot these $(x,y)$ pairs on the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/c3/c37a098fd2295afed0d1793286f0d36f7d2bd452e25c3df2b754c6ee50621a1f.png)\n\nFinally, connecting the points gives us a straight line. This is the graph of $y=-x+2.$\n\n![](images/b6/b6d9743048b7b3ef7a0c26ac21df36c45e2b3a044fe738b875ac2883fb62d5ea.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186618047 :question/problem "Copy and complete the table below and use it to determine which of the following is the graph of $y=2x-2.$\n\n| $x$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "First, we fill the table. Given a value of $x,$ we can find the corresponding $y$ by substituting $x$ into the formula $y=2x-2.$ Therefore,\n\n- if $x=2$ then $y=2(2)-2=2,$\n\n- if $x=3$ then $y=2(3)-2=4,$\n\n- if $x=4$ then $y=2(4)-2=6,$ and\n\n- if $x=5$ then $y=2(5)-2=8.$\n\n| $x$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ |\n\nWe plot these $(x,y)$ pairs on the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/f3/f380135b711cadd50e16ad9e1121c558b205d11536947f1adbc8e767f015f068.png)\n\nFinally, connecting the points gives us a straight line. This is the graph of $y=2x-2.$\n\n![](images/34/34e908221ed8aa5608732cc28b19a23cb42ef2ff1ae18cf8091648657cab795b.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186619730 :question/problem "![](images/c5/c5fa93d7d0c2e1f13df0235b7f250f0a4dfd2216424043b674540de00d05baa1.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate the ellipse shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(-2,-3),$ $a=6$ and $b=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186619768 :question/problem "The Cartesian equation of the ellipse defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +5,\\,y=2\\sqrt{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -9,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +5 \\\\ \\frac{x-5}{7} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-5)^{2}}{{7}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-5)^{2}}{49} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2\\sqrt{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -9 \\\\ \\frac{y+9}{2\\sqrt{10}} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+9)^{2}}{(2\\sqrt{10})^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+9)^{2}}{40} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-5)^{2}}{49}+\\frac{(y+9)^{2}}{40} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-5)^{2}}{49}+\\frac{(y+9)^{2}}{40}=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186619791 :question/problem "Given that $\\lambda (\\frac{2}{7}\\mathbf{a}+\\frac{7}{2}\\mathbf{b})$ and $4\\mathbf{a}+49\\mathbf{b}$ are equal (non-zero) vectors, find the scale factor $\\lambda .$" :question/worked-solution "Since the vectors are equal, we can write\n\n$$\n4a+49b=\\lambda (\\frac{2}{7}a+\\frac{7}{2}b).\n$$\n\nNext, we factor the left-hand side, which gives\n\n$$\n14(\\frac{2}{7}a+\\frac{7}{2}b)=\\lambda (\\frac{2}{7}a+\\frac{7}{2}b).\n$$\n\nNotice that we have $(\\frac{2}{7}a+\\frac{7}{2}b)$ on both sides. So, let's define a new vector $u$ as\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{2}{7}a+\\frac{7}{2}b.\n$$\n\nThen, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n14u=\\lambda u.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\lambda =14.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186619799 :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=3{y}^{2}-4{x}^{2}$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2})$, can be found by computing $∇f(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{y}^{2}-4{x}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{y}^{2}-4{x}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =-8x\\,i+6y\\,j \\\\ ∇f(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}) & =-8\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)i+6\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)j \\\\  & =-4\\,i+3\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2})}{‖∇f(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2})‖} & =\\frac{(-4\\,i+3\\,j)}{\\sqrt{{(-4)}^{2}+{(3)}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-4\\,i+3\\,j)}{\\sqrt{16+9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-4\\,i+3\\,j)}{\\sqrt{25}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(-4\\,i+3\\,j).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186620824 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=⟨2,0,x⟩$, $\\mathbf{b}=⟨3,2,x⟩$ and $|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}|=\\sqrt{61}.$ Find $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 2 & 0 & x \\\\ 3 & 2 & x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & x \\\\ 2 & x\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & x \\\\ 3 & x\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(0-2x)\\mathbf{i}-(2x-3x)\\mathbf{j}+(4-0)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-2x,x,4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the magnitude, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{(-2x)^{2}+{x}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+{x}^{2}+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5{x}^{2}+16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we solve for $x$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{61} \\\\ \\sqrt{5{x}^{2}+16} & =\\sqrt{61} \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+16 & =61 \\\\ 5{x}^{2} & =45 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=\\pm 3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186620854 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=⟨x,3,6⟩,$ $\\mathbf{b}=⟨2,1,2⟩$ and $|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}|=\\sqrt{80}.$ Then, in **ascending order**, the possible values of $x$ are\n\n$\\,x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ x & 3 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}x & 6 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}x & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(6-6)\\mathbf{i}-(2x-12)\\mathbf{j}+(x-6)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨0,12-2x,x-6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the magnitude, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(12-2x)^{2}+(x-6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{144-48x+4{x}^{2}+{x}^{2}-12x+36} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5{x}^{2}-60x+180}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we solve for $x$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{80} \\\\ \\sqrt{5{x}^{2}-50x+180} & =\\sqrt{80} \\\\ 5{x}^{2}-60x+180 & =80 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}-60x+100 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-12x+20 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)(x-10) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=2,$ $x=10.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186620984 :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=(2 x^{2} - 3)^{4},$ find the slope at the point $(1,1)$" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use the chain rule to find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\nFirst, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf=g^{4},\\,g=2 x^{2} - 3\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=4g^{3},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=4 x.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} &=4g^{3}\\cdot (4 x) \\\\  &=16 x(2 x^{2} - 3)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line at the point where $x=1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|_{x=1} &=[16 x(2 x^{2} - 3)^{3}]|_{x=1} \\\\  &=16 (1)(2 (1)^{2} - 3)^{3} \\\\  &=-16.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186620993 :question/problem "![](images/51/5126c74f997dc78e8a14e444b16999781f57f9a3f09db40fd185592195f2cb77.png)\n\nIdentify the points that **DO NOT** lie on the line $y=-2x-4$?" :question/worked-solution "The plotted points correspond to the $xy$-pairs $A(-1,-3)$, $B(-2,3)$, $C(-3,2)$.\n\nTo check whether a point lies on the line, we substitute the coordinates of the point into the equation and determine whether the resulting statement is true.\n\n- Substituting the point $A(-1,-3)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= -2x-4 \\\\ (-3) &= -2(-1)-4 \\\\ -3 &= -2 && \\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $A$ does not lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $B(-2,3)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= -2x-4 \\\\ (3) &= -2(-2)-4 \\\\ 3 &= 0 && \\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $B$ does not lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $C(-3,2)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= -2x-4 \\\\ (2) &= -2(-3)-4 \\\\ 2 &= 2 && \\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $C$ does lie on the line.\n\nFinally, only points $A$ and $B$ do not lie on the line."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186620998 :question/problem "![](images/58/5826c4d6340af4a652a2bac5260c51e6b6e9ecf95478627be1705f0cf5b1d727.png)\n\nIdentify the points that **DO NOT** lie on the line $y=-3x+4$." :question/worked-solution "The plotted points correspond to the $xy$-pairs $A(0,3)$, $B(3,-5)$, $C(2,-2)$.\n\nTo check whether a point lies on the line, we substitute the coordinates of the point into the equation and determine whether the resulting statement is true.\n\n- Substituting the point $A(0,3)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= -3x+4 \\\\ (3) &= -3(0)+4 \\\\ 3 &= 4 && \\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $A$ does not lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $B(3,-5)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= -3x+4 \\\\ (-5) &= -3(3)+4 \\\\ -5 &= -5 && \\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $B$ does lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $C(2,-2)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= -3x+4 \\\\ (-2) &= -3(2)+4 \\\\ -2 &= -2 && \\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $C$ does lie on the line.\n\nFinally, only point $A$ does not lie on the line."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186621001 :question/problem "![](images/11/11423dab0f796e898be878520cf136c1130273aa66f01639b37e5f949785d6d6.png)\n\nIdentify the points that **DO NOT** lie on the line $y=-2x+2$." :question/worked-solution "The plotted points correspond to the $xy$-pairs $A(-2,6)$, $B(2,-4)$, $C(-1,7)$.\n\nTo check whether a point lies on the line, we substitute the coordinates of the point into the equation and determine whether the resulting statement is true.\n\n- Substituting the point $A(-2,6)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= -2x+2 \\\\ (6) &= -2(-2)+2 \\\\ 6 &= 6 && \\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $A$ does lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $B(2,-4)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= -2x+2 \\\\ (-4) &= -2(2)+2 \\\\ -4 &= -2 && \\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $B$ does not lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $C(-1,7)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= -2x+2 \\\\ (7) &= -2(-1)+2 \\\\ 7 &= 4 && \\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $C$ does not lie on the line.\n\nFinally, only points $B$ and $C$ do not lie on the line."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186621048 :question/problem "Copy and complete the table below and use it to determine which of the following is the graph of $y=-4x-2.$\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "First, we fill the table. Given a value of $x,$ we can find the corresponding $y$ by substituting $x$ into the formula $y=-4x-2.$ Therefore,\n\n- if $x=1$ then $y=-4(1)-2=-6,$\n\n- if $x=2$ then $y=-4(2)-2=-10,$\n\n- if $x=3$ then $y=-4(3)-2=-14,$ and\n\n- if $x=4$ then $y=-4(4)-2=-18.$\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $-6$ | $-10$ | $-14$ | $-18$ |\n\nWe plot these $(x,y)$ pairs on the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/d2/d2be7541030a65f678da5971d797579a0c6081cec346be25a879116fdbd6f72c.png)\n\nFinally, connecting the points gives us a straight line. This is the graph of $y=-4x-2.$\n\n![](images/4c/4ca1fa078f7f6d8136a030e7ec44adf796ed06481ff3aaafbefe74d88a5a6c15.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186621050 :question/problem "Copy and complete the table below and use it to determine which of the following is the graph of $y=-3x-2.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "First, we fill the table. Given a value of $x,$ we can find the corresponding $y$ by substituting $x$ into the formula $y=-3x-2.$ Therefore,\n\n- if $x=0$ then $y=-3(0)-2=-2,$\n\n- if $x=1$ then $y=-3(1)-2=-5,$\n\n- if $x=2$ then $y=-3(2)-2=-8,$ and\n\n- if $x=3$ then $y=-3(3)-2=-11.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $-2$ | $-5$ | $-8$ | $-11$ |\n\nWe plot these $(x,y)$ pairs on the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/51/51adf104900ef3b01fe71d15aa250ed872805fbbaecf7a0382a6ce6f2f1c603f.png)\n\nFinally, connecting the points gives us a straight line. This is the graph of $y=-3x-2.$\n\n![](images/2d/2d388d5b2240d45b1a79abe1c4c3e04002a515f613ca87e2ab4e5b131b26cbcb.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186621057 :question/problem "Copy and complete the table below and use it to determine which of the following is the graph of $y=-4x+5.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "First, we fill the table. Given a value of $x,$ we can find the corresponding $y$ by substituting $x$ into the formula $y=-4x+5.$ Therefore,\n\n- if $x=0$ then $y=-4(0)+5=5,$\n\n- if $x=1$ then $y=-4(1)+5=1,$\n\n- if $x=2$ then $y=-4(2)+5=-3,$ and\n\n- if $x=3$ then $y=-4(3)+5=-7.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $5$ | $1$ | $-3$ | $-7$ |\n\nWe plot these $(x,y)$ pairs on the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/b9/b98f86cec2f28d1eecf50c09eb625837cb8a0a6cbdd83960ffd4ffd34ff9df9d.png)\n\nFinally, connecting the points gives us a straight line. This is the graph of $y=-4x+5.$\n\n![](images/49/493363ba9b516b87855da69fcf796f7d9b5ff6cb74548c08e82138ae84040e8b.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186622023 :question/problem "Let $a=\\langle 6,-4\\rangle$. Find $|\\,a\\,|$." :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n|\\,a\\,| &= \\sqrt{a_x^2+a_y^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(6)^2+(-4)^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{36+16} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{52} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4\\cdot 13} \\\\\n&= 2 \\sqrt{13}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186622035 :question/problem "If $u=\\langle -10,6\\rangle$, then what is the magnitude of $u$?" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n|\\,u\\,| &= \\sqrt{u_x^2+u_y^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(-10)^2+(6)^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{100+36} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{136} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4\\cdot 34} \\\\\n&= 2 \\sqrt{34}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186622049 :question/problem "Let $a=\\langle 4,10\\rangle$. Find $|\\,a\\,|$." :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n|\\,a\\,| &= \\sqrt{a_x^2+a_y^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(4)^2+(10)^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{16+100} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{116} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4\\cdot 29} \\\\\n&= 2 \\sqrt{29}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186622050 :question/problem "If $u=\\langle 6,-5\\rangle$, then what is the magnitude of $u$?" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n|\\,u\\,| &= \\sqrt{u_x^2+u_y^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(6)^2+(-5)^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{36+25} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{61}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186622051 :question/problem "If $a=\\langle -4,1\\rangle$, then what is the magnitude of $a$?" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n|\\,a\\,| &= \\sqrt{a_x^2+a_y^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(-4)^2+(1)^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{16+1} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{17}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186622064 :question/problem "![](images/92/925bbc9a27faad62d5672c40bfe15ae47d3daed9dca2c307aea97f8d59742f6d.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the graph of the straight line above?\n\nI. $y-\\frac{2}{3}x=-3$\n\nII. $- \\frac{3}{2}y+x=\\frac{9}{2}$\n\nIII. $y=\\frac{2}{3}x-4$" :question/worked-solution "Let's pick two distinct points on the line. For example, $A$ and $B$ with coordinates $(0,-3)$ and $(3,-1)$, respectively.\n\n![](images/2f/2fd4e2ba95ed99c626122fb351b6df0d7bf78dd750a565d84e135689062007c0.png)\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of both points in our equations. If both points satisfy an equation, then we can conclude that the equation represents the line in the graph. Otherwise, if any point does *not* satisfy the equation, then we can conclude that the equation does *not* represent the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation I: $y-\\frac{2}{3}x=-3.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,-3)$ and $B(3,-1)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-3)-\\frac{2}{3}(0) &=-3 \\\\ -3 &=-3\\,\\checkmark \\\\ (-1)-\\frac{2}{3}(3) &=-3 \\\\ -3 &=-3\\,\\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y-\\frac{2}{3}x=-3$ is an equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation II: $- \\frac{3}{2}y+x=\\frac{9}{2}.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,-3)$ and $B(3,-1)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}- \\frac{3}{2}(-3)+(0) &=\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ \\frac{9}{2} &=\\frac{9}{2}\\,\\checkmark \\\\ - \\frac{3}{2}(-1)+(3) &=\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ \\frac{9}{2} &=\\frac{9}{2}\\,\\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $- \\frac{3}{2}y+x=\\frac{9}{2}$ is an equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation III: $y=\\frac{2}{3}x-4.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,-3)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-3) &=\\frac{2}{3}(0)-4 \\\\ -3 &=-4\\,\\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=\\frac{2}{3}x-4$ is *not* an equation of the line in the graph.\n\nOf the given equations, the equations that represented the line in the graph were $y-\\frac{2}{3}x=-3$, $- \\frac{3}{2}y+x=\\frac{9}{2}$, corresponding to I, II. Therefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186622065 :question/problem "![](images/d3/d33c96c388c19a4bd5b9c575d75a5ab1c4d53071b8279fd9d5eae60c6dbed8a4.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the graph of the straight line above?\n\nI. $y=-2x-2$\n\nII. $y=-2+\\frac{1}{2}x$\n\nIII. $y+\\frac{1}{2}x=-2$" :question/worked-solution "Let's pick two distinct points on the line. For example, $A$ and $B$ with coordinates $(0,-2)$ and $(2,-3)$, respectively.\n\n![](images/4e/4e51f973e8c91836e7b3d762e175e3941b45dcc10dd805984c743bec90b456b0.png)\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of both points in our equations. If both points satisfy an equation, then we can conclude that the equation represents the line in the graph. Otherwise, if any point does *not* satisfy the equation, then we can conclude that the equation does *not* represent the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation I: $y=-2x-2.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,-2)$ and $B(2,-3)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2) &=-2(0)-2 \\\\ -2 &=-2\\,\\checkmark \\\\ (-3) &=-2(2)-2 \\\\ -3 &=-6\\,\\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=-2x-2$ is *not* an equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation II: $y=-2+\\frac{1}{2}x.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,-2)$ and $B(2,-3)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2) &=-2+\\frac{1}{2}(0) \\\\ -2 &=-2\\,\\checkmark \\\\ (-3) &=-2+\\frac{1}{2}(2) \\\\ -3 &=-1\\,\\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=-2+\\frac{1}{2}x$ is *not* an equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation III: $y+\\frac{1}{2}x=-2.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,-2)$ and $B(2,-3)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2)+\\frac{1}{2}(0) &=-2 \\\\ -2 &=-2\\,\\checkmark \\\\ (-3)+\\frac{1}{2}(2) &=-2 \\\\ -2 &=-2\\,\\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y+\\frac{1}{2}x=-2$ is an equation of the line in the graph.\n\nOf the given equations, the equations that represented the line in the graph were $y+\\frac{1}{2}x=-2$, corresponding to III. Therefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186622069 :question/problem "![](images/75/7542265fbd9974f33a1d4e9124dbd2df61eb15ed30f61a3399abe8996550a6bb.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the graph of the straight line above?\n\nI. $y=\\frac{3}{2}x-1$\n\nII. $y=0+\\frac{3}{2}x$\n\nIII. $y-\\frac{3}{2}x=-1$" :question/worked-solution "Let's pick two distinct points on the line. For example, $A$ and $B$ with coordinates $(0,-1)$ and $(2,2)$, respectively.\n\n![](images/ca/ca567dbc547d11cf337835ed7d7213a3e2c5e9b513fce544f7bcad21177ddacc.png)\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of both points in our equations. If both points satisfy an equation, then we can conclude that the equation represents the line in the graph. Otherwise, if any point does *not* satisfy the equation, then we can conclude that the equation does *not* represent the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation I: $y=\\frac{3}{2}x-1.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,-1)$ and $B(2,2)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1) &=\\frac{3}{2}(0)-1 \\\\ -1 &=-1\\,\\checkmark \\\\ (2) &=\\frac{3}{2}(2)-1 \\\\ 2 &=2\\,\\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=\\frac{3}{2}x-1$ is an equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation II: $y=0+\\frac{3}{2}x.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,-1)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1) &=0+\\frac{3}{2}(0) \\\\ -1 &=0\\,\\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=0+\\frac{3}{2}x$ is *not* an equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation III: $y-\\frac{3}{2}x=-1.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,-1)$ and $B(2,2)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)-\\frac{3}{2}(0) &=-1 \\\\ -1 &=-1\\,\\checkmark \\\\ (2)-\\frac{3}{2}(2) &=-1 \\\\ -1 &=-1\\,\\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y-\\frac{3}{2}x=-1$ is an equation of the line in the graph.\n\nOf the given equations, the equations that represented the line in the graph were $y=\\frac{3}{2}x-1$, $y-\\frac{3}{2}x=-1$, corresponding to I, III. Therefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186622075 :question/problem "![](images/40/40b5efdcecdc520292c5f5be9047b061b3eb5da2b869be81397b26aed8a6d620.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the graph of the straight line above?\n\nI. $y+\\frac{3}{2}x=3$\n\nII. $\\frac{3}{2}y+x=3$\n\nIII. $y=- \\frac{3}{2}x+2$" :question/worked-solution "Let's pick two distinct points on the line. For example, $A$ and $B$ with coordinates $(0,2)$ and $(2,-1)$, respectively.\n\n![](images/83/83f23046b24dcdcfee122662efddc944303865a368fd3fb54eca005fcb631fe7.png)\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of both points in our equations. If both points satisfy an equation, then we can conclude that the equation represents the line in the graph. Otherwise, if any point does *not* satisfy the equation, then we can conclude that the equation does *not* represent the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation I: $y+\\frac{3}{2}x=3.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,2)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2)+\\frac{3}{2}(0) &=3 \\\\ 2 &=3\\,\\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y+\\frac{3}{2}x=3$ is *not* an equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation II: $\\frac{3}{2}y+x=3.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,2)$ and $B(2,-1)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}(2)+(0) &=3 \\\\ 3 &=3\\,\\checkmark \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}(-1)+(2) &=3 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} &=3\\,\\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{3}{2}y+x=3$ is *not* an equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation III: $y=- \\frac{3}{2}x+2.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,2)$ and $B(2,-1)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2) &=- \\frac{3}{2}(0)+2 \\\\ 2 &=2\\,\\checkmark \\\\ (-1) &=- \\frac{3}{2}(2)+2 \\\\ -1 &=-1\\,\\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=- \\frac{3}{2}x+2$ is an equation of the line in the graph.\n\nOf the given equations, the equations that represented the line in the graph were $y=- \\frac{3}{2}x+2$, corresponding to III. Therefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186622089 :question/problem "![](images/97/971525a7902dac13a95d27a5a749888588f833f6d00c8eb666b11ed3fd723df9.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the graph of the straight line above?\n\nI. $y=- \\frac{3}{2}x-3$\n\nII. $y=-3+\\frac{2}{3}x$\n\nIII. $y+\\frac{2}{3}x=-3$" :question/worked-solution "Let's pick two distinct points on the line. For example, $A$ and $B$ with coordinates $(0,-3)$ and $(3,-5)$, respectively.\n\n![](images/b6/b66a54a81055f919d8c5f375ffae2c8e7fc4e4a5ecd14ba2b405c956614e7884.png)\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of both points in our equations. If both points satisfy an equation, then we can conclude that the equation represents the line in the graph. Otherwise, if any point does *not* satisfy the equation, then we can conclude that the equation does *not* represent the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation I: $y=- \\frac{3}{2}x-3.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,-3)$ and $B(3,-5)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-3) &=- \\frac{3}{2}(0)-3 \\\\ -3 &=-3\\,\\checkmark \\\\ (-5) &=- \\frac{3}{2}(3)-3 \\\\ -5 &=- \\frac{15}{2}\\,\\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=- \\frac{3}{2}x-3$ is *not* an equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation II: $y=-3+\\frac{2}{3}x.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,-3)$ and $B(3,-5)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-3) &=-3+\\frac{2}{3}(0) \\\\ -3 &=-3\\,\\checkmark \\\\ (-5) &=-3+\\frac{2}{3}(3) \\\\ -5 &=-1\\,\\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=-3+\\frac{2}{3}x$ is *not* an equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation III: $y+\\frac{2}{3}x=-3.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,-3)$ and $B(3,-5)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-3)+\\frac{2}{3}(0) &=-3 \\\\ -3 &=-3\\,\\checkmark \\\\ (-5)+\\frac{2}{3}(3) &=-3 \\\\ -3 &=-3\\,\\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y+\\frac{2}{3}x=-3$ is an equation of the line in the graph.\n\nOf the given equations, the equations that represented the line in the graph were $y+\\frac{2}{3}x=-3$, corresponding to III. Therefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186622116 :question/problem "Which of the following graphs shows the line $y=-5-2x?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find two distinct points that lie on our line. Any two points will do!\n\n- For the first point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=0.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-5-2x \\\\  & =-5-2(0) \\\\  & =-5-0 \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first point is $(0,-5).$\n\n- For the second point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=-2.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-5-2x \\\\  & =-5-2(-2) \\\\  & =-5+4 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second point is $(-2,-1).$\n\nFinally, we plot those two points on the $xy$-plane and draw a line through them. This is the graph of $y=-5-2x.$\n\n![](images/7c/7c1acc4c8a5aaf32eca945aabdda84f45fe35b62065c290dcdf75198bdf0f073.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186622125 :question/problem "Which of the following graphs shows the line $y=2x-5?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find two distinct points that lie on our line. Any two points will do!\n\n- For the first point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=0.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2x-5 \\\\  & =2(0)-5 \\\\  & =0-5 \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first point is $(0,-5).$\n\n- For the second point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=-2.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2x-5 \\\\  & =2(-2)-5 \\\\  & =-4-5 \\\\  & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second point is $(-2,-9).$\n\nFinally, we plot those two points on the $xy$-plane and draw a line through them. This is the graph of $y=2x-5.$\n\n![](images/a6/a65be51e1c9abaf82f2bd7f905936ed5550602b0fb7b5c208047684e750c3c30.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186622140 :question/problem "Which of the following graphs shows the line $y=x-5?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find two distinct points that lie on our line. Any two points will do!\n\n- For the first point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=0.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x-5 \\\\  & =(0)-5 \\\\  & =0-5 \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first point is $(0,-5).$\n\n- For the second point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=2.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x-5 \\\\  & =(2)-5 \\\\  & =2-5 \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second point is $(2,-3).$\n\nFinally, we plot those two points on the $xy$-plane and draw a line through them. This is the graph of $y=x-5.$\n\n![](images/2e/2edc9ee1cc0dd6b0b8b57d60f892146d36383e751a2226d4717fc465cbb70fd8.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186622142 :question/problem "Which of the following graphs shows the line $y=-x?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find two distinct points that lie on our line. Any two points will do!\n\n- For the first point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=0.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-x \\\\  & =-(0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first point is $(0,0).$\n\n- For the second point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=1.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-x \\\\  & =-(1) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second point is $(1,-1).$\n\nFinally, we plot those two points on the $xy$-plane and draw a line through them. This is the graph of $y=-x.$ \n\n![](images/a4/a498da6031f3b5b2a67230282604884b65204b1fa6dcc2126445692729852c6c.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186622143 :question/problem "Which of the following graphs shows the line $y=4+2x?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find two distinct points that lie on our line. Any two points will do!\n\n- For the first point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=0.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4+2x \\\\  & =4+2(0) \\\\  & =4+0 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first point is $(0,4).$\n\n- For the second point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=1.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4+2x \\\\  & =4+2(1) \\\\  & =4+2 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second point is $(1,6).$\n\nFinally, we plot those two points on the $xy$-plane and draw a line through them. This is the graph of $y=4+2x.$\n\n![](images/7a/7a3554247a7d77993c122737cd4ac3f67b12ac0745f00038f2bf9a5dd6bb77be.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186622804 :question/problem "Find $\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})$ given that $\\mathbf{a}=⟨-\\sqrt{3},\\hspace{0.167em}-6,\\hspace{0.167em}0⟩$ and $\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}=⟨-\\sqrt{3},\\hspace{0.167em}5,\\hspace{0.167em}9⟩.$\n\n$\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Substituting given vectors into the formula for the scalar triple product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =⟨-\\sqrt{3},\\hspace{0.167em}-6,\\hspace{0.167em}0⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\sqrt{3},\\hspace{0.167em}5,\\hspace{0.167em}9⟩ \\\\  & =(-\\sqrt{3})(-\\sqrt{3})+(-6)(5)+(0)(9) \\\\  & =3-30+0 \\\\  & =-27.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186622816 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{p}=⟨6,4,6⟩,$ $\\mathbf{q}=⟨5,0,2⟩$ and $\\mathbf{r}=⟨-1,4,-2⟩.$ Find $\\mathbf{r}\\cdot (\\mathbf{p}\\times \\mathbf{q}).$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the scalar triple product $\\mathbf{r}\\cdot (\\mathbf{p}\\times \\mathbf{q})$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $\\mathbf{r},$ $\\mathbf{p},$ and $\\mathbf{q}.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}\\cdot (\\mathbf{p}\\times \\mathbf{q}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-4\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}6 & 6 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+(-2)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}6 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-1)(4\\cdot 2-0\\cdot 6)-4(6\\cdot 2-5\\cdot 6)-2(6\\cdot 0-5\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =-8+72+40 \\\\  & =104.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186622823 :question/problem "Calculate the volume $V$ of the parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD},$ given the points $A(0,-1,2),$ $B(-2,4,-1),$ $C(-4,-2,0),$ and $D(-2,3,1).$\n\n$V=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(-2i+4j-k)-(-j+2k) \\\\  & =-2i+5j-3k \\\\  & =⟨-2,5,-3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(-4i-2j)-(-j+2k) \\\\  & =-4i-j-2k \\\\  & =⟨-4,-1,-2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =d-a \\\\  & =(-2i+3j+k)-(-j+2k) \\\\  & =-2i+4j-k \\\\  & =⟨-2,4,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 5 & -3 \\\\ -4 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{vmatrix}-5\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}+(-3)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-4 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2((-1)(-1)-4(-2))-5((-4)(-1)-(-2)(-2))-3((-4)(4)-(-2)(-1)) \\\\  & =-18-0+54 \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelpiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|36|=36.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186622827 :question/problem "Find $\\mathbf{b}\\cdot (\\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{a}),$ if $\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}=⟨7,-2,-3⟩$ and $\\mathbf{a}=⟨1,7,5⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the triple scalar product \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =⟨1,7,5⟩\\cdot ⟨7,-2,-3⟩ \\\\  & =1\\cdot 7+7\\cdot (-2)+5\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =7-14-15 \\\\  & =-22.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{b}\\cdot (\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{c}) &=22\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand again we swap $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{b}\\cdot (\\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{a}) &=-22.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186622834 :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the parallelepiped spanned by $\\mathbf{x}=⟨-1,3,-2⟩,$ $\\mathbf{y}=⟨4,0,3⟩,$ and $\\mathbf{z}=⟨7,7,4⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "The volume of the parallelepiped spanned by three vectors is given by the absolute value of their triple product.\n\nFirst, we compute the triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot (y\\times z) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 7 & 7 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 7 & 4\\end{vmatrix}-3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 7 & 4\\end{vmatrix}+(-2)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 7\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-1)(0\\cdot 4-7\\cdot 3)-3(4\\cdot 4-7\\cdot 3)-2(4\\cdot 7-7\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =21+15-56 \\\\  & =-20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nFinally, we take the absolute value. So the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped is \n\n$$\nV=|-20|=20.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186622835 :question/problem "Find $\\mathbf{c}\\cdot (\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b})$, if $\\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{b}=⟨-4,6,3⟩$ and $\\mathbf{a}=⟨2,5,-1⟩.$\n\n$\\mathbf{c}\\cdot (\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b})=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the triple scalar product \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{b}) & =⟨2,5,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨-4,6,3⟩ \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-4)+5\\cdot 6+(-1)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-8+30-3 \\\\  & =19.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{c}\\cdot (\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}) &=-19.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186622836 :question/problem "Find $k$ given that the vectors $\\mathbf{p}=⟨1,0,k⟩$, $\\mathbf{q}=⟨2,3,1⟩$, and $\\mathbf{r}=⟨0,4,5⟩$ are coplanar.\n\n$k=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\np\\cdot (q\\times r)=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & k \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 5\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{vmatrix}-0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{vmatrix}+k\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(3\\cdot 5-1\\cdot 4)+0+k(2\\cdot 4-3\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =11+8k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot (q\\times r) & =0 \\\\ 11+8k & =0 \\\\ 8k & =-11 \\\\ k & =-\\frac{11}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=-\\frac{11}{8}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186623649 :question/problem "Calculate the area ${A}_{△}$ of the triangle spanned by $\\mathbf{c}=⟨1,0,2⟩$ and $\\mathbf{d}=⟨0,3,-1⟩.$\n\n${A}_{△}=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\times d & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(0\\cdot (-1)-2\\cdot 3)i-(1\\cdot (-1)-2\\cdot 0)j+(1\\cdot 3-0\\cdot 0)k \\\\  & =-6i+j+3k \\\\  & =⟨-6,1,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the corresponding parallelogram is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\,c\\times d\\,| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-6)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36+1+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{46}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of the triangle is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{46}}{2}=\\frac{\\sqrt{46}}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186623687 :question/problem "Calculate the area of the $△\\,JKL,$ if $J(2,0,1),$ $K(5,2,3),$ $L(1,3,0).$\n\n${A}_{△}=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{JK}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{JL}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{JK} & =k-j \\\\  & =(5i+2j+3k)-(2i+k) \\\\  & =3i+2j+2k \\\\  & =⟨3,2,2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{JL} & =l-j \\\\  & =(i+3j)-(2i+k) \\\\  & =-i+3j-k \\\\  & =⟨-1,3,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{JK}\\times \\overset{\\to }{JL} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(2\\cdot (-1)-2\\cdot 3)i-(3\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot 2)j+(3\\cdot 3-2\\cdot (-1))k \\\\  & =-8i+j+11k \\\\  & =⟨-8,1,11⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{JK}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{JL}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{JK}\\times \\overset{\\to }{JL}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-8)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{11}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{186}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,JKL$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{186}}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186623787 :question/problem "![](images/dc/dc355ab2667e4f215b6c7b27d00029c1c164cc054829fbc72dba82786c173810.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the graph of the straight line above?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's pick two distinct points on the line. For example, $A$ and $B$ with coordinates $(0,1)$ and $(1,0)$, respectively.\n\n![](images/de/def3b4d24e1db68fbd7e872f786fa8e20e4ff4dc193ed3060c08d1b12dfd5c5e.png)\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of both points in our equations. If both points satisfy an equation, then we can conclude that the equation represents the line in the graph. Otherwise, if any point does *not* satisfy the equation, then we can conclude that the equation does *not* represent the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation: $y=-x.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,1)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1) &=-(0) \\\\ 1 &=0\\,\\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=-x$ is *not* an equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation: $y=1-x.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,1)$ and $B(1,0)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1) &=1-(0) \\\\ 1 &=1\\,\\checkmark \\\\ (0) &=1-(1) \\\\ 0 &=0\\,\\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=1-x$ is an equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation: $y+x=1.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,1)$ and $B(1,0)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1)+(0) &=1 \\\\ 1 &=1\\,\\checkmark \\\\ (0)+(1) &=1 \\\\ 1 &=1\\,\\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y+x=1$ is an equation of the line in the graph.\n\nSelect $y=1-x$, $y+x=1$."}]}
